+# BLOCKED: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz checksum mismatch
+
+## What is blocking
+
+The release check cannot pass without deciding which value is authoritative for
+`artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`, because the artifact on disk does not match the
+digest recorded in `SHA256SUMS`.
+
+```
+manifest:  7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD
+actual:    FDE9B2575EDBEFB297FA7D93738B4BB1F09B7F777D1723B90194AD53CD9BC9CE
+```
+
+The other two artifacts match their manifest entries:
+
+```
+61EBDFF87C1DBE732D97CF4291AE26E0A2020E7C77C833BC16273B8CF412F9A8  cli-2.0.1.zip
+EFB784621E881704133D7FD4E637995F8F96B87E03B908C1CB4EE227E8A70FD9  docs-2026.10.tar.gz
+```
+
+## Why I did not "fix" it
+
+`SHA256SUMS` is the integrity reference. Editing it to match the current bytes
+would make the check pass while destroying its purpose: it would silently bless
+whatever is currently in `artifacts/`, including a possibly corrupted, truncated,
+or tampered artifact. The same applies to fabricating a replacement tarball.
+
+There is no source/build input in this repository from which `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`
+could be regenerated, and the artifact is not a readable archive (it is only
+random-looking payload bytes), so the correct content cannot be reconstructed or
+validated here. Only the artifact's owner/publisher can say which side is right.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two genuine bugs that also blocked the check:
+
+1. It opened artifacts in text mode and hashed `read().encode()`, which both
+   corrupts the bytes and raises `UnicodeDecodeError` on binary artifacts.
+   It now opens them with `"rb"` and hashes the raw bytes.
+2. It compared the lowercase `hexdigest()` against the uppercase digests in
+   `SHA256SUMS` verbatim, so every entry would mismatch. Comparison is now
+   case-insensitive (`expected.lower()`).
+
+With those fixes, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass, and
+`python3 verify.py` reports only the one genuinely bad file above.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Do one of the following, then re-run `python3 verify.py`:
+
+- Confirm the artifact is wrong: rebuild/republish `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from
+  the release source and confirm its SHA-256 is
+  `7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD`.
+- Confirm the manifest is wrong: verify the current artifact out-of-band (e.g.
+  against the build log or publisher's recorded hash), then deliberately update
+  its `SHA256SUMS` entry to
+  `FDE9B2575EDBEFB297FA7D93738B4BB1F09B7F777D1723B90194AD53CD9BC9CE`
+  and document why.
